{-
   `@convention`, `@x86_legacy_sse` and `X86.vzeroupper()` - the three markers that make a legacy-SSE
   region, asserted where they are written.

   # extensions: sha v3

   What they are for is one hazard: the SHA extension has no VEX spelling in the architecture, so a
   function holding one of its instructions crosses between prefixed and unprefixed encodings every
   few instructions unless the whole body is unprefixed - which costs 140x on the part it was
   measured on - and even then can be *entered* with an upper half left dirty by its caller, which
   costs a further ~160 ns per call. See `legacyVectorEncodings` in codegen/x64/target.h for the
   first number and `test/bench/shamix/` for the second.

   The three markers answer that as three separate facts, and keeping them separate is the point:

     - `@x86_legacy_sse` is about *encoding* and goes on every function of the region, helpers
       included, because the crossing is between two instructions and a call does not stop it.
     - `X86.vzeroupper()` is the state change itself, and it is a *call* rather than something the
       attribute implies - because only the region's entry points want it, and a rule deriving which
       ones from a signature would stop being right the moment one changed.

   **The convention is `sysv` and not `clobber`**, which an earlier design had. What the reset needs
   is a convention with no callee-saved *vector* register; `clobber` keeps nothing at all, which
   costs the caller a `push`/`pop` pair per preserved register and buys nothing more. System V's
   vector file is caller-saved by the psABI, which is a promise that outlives any decision this
   compiler makes about its own conventions - `Complex` satisfies the condition today and is not
   obliged to keep doing so.

   Both halves of the safety are checked rather than assumed, in `checkLegacyVectorEncoding`: a
   function calling the intrinsic may not be entered under a convention preserving a vector register,
   and may not hold a *wide* vector value. Wide, because `vzeroupper` zeroes bits 128 and up and
   leaves the low half alone - a `Vec(U32, 4)` is an xmm and survives, which is why the helper below
   may take one.

   What is asserted here is that all three reach the backend from source at all: before this, no Yana
   declaration could name a convention - `LowerFunction::callType` had exactly one setter, the
   lower-IR text parser - and the region was decided by a pass walking the call graph instead.

   The bytes `vzeroupper` encodes to are pinned separately, in `test/x64/VectorReset.lower`, and the
   library that uses all three is `lib/Digest/Hardware.sha.yana`.
-}

import Native

{-
   The region's entry point, with all three markers.

   `@noinline` is written out rather than implied by `@convention`, and it is load-bearing: the
   convention and the reset describe a *call boundary*, and an inlined function has none. It is on
   the declaration that depends on it rather than folded into a general attribute, so that a future
   `@convention(sysv)` does not inherit an inlining decision it has no opinion about.
-}
@noinline
@convention(clobber)
@x86_legacy_sse
fn compressLike(state: Vec(U32, 4), message: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Int:
    X86.vzeroupper()

    -- A SHA instruction, which is what makes this region a region: it has no VEX spelling, so the
    -- `add` and the lane read around it must not have one either.
    let mixed = X86.sha256Message1(state, message)
    return bitcast(lane(mixed + state, 0)) :: Int

-- A helper of the same region: the encoding attribute and nothing else. It takes a vector argument,
-- which is exactly why it must not clear the state - and why `@x86_legacy_sse` says only one thing.
@x86_legacy_sse
fn helper(words: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4) = X86.sha256Message2(words, words)

@noinline
@convention(clobber)
@x86_legacy_sse
fn throughHelper(state: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Int:
    X86.vzeroupper()
    return bitcast(lane(helper(state), 0)) :: Int

{-
   And a reset outside any of it.

   Legal, and worth one line: the instruction is not tied to the attribute in either direction. A
   function may clear the vector state without being encoded legacy - what it costs is three bytes -
   and the check that holds a *marked* function to its promise has nothing to say about this one.
-}
fn plainReset(x: Int) -> Int:
    X86.vzeroupper()
    return x + 1

fn main() -> Int:
    let a = splat(1 :: U32) :: Vec(U32, 4)
    let b = splat(2 :: U32) :: Vec(U32, 4)

    return compressLike(a, b) + throughHelper(a) + plainReset(0)
